# rule.require.boundary-qualified-terms

> a term carries its FULL boundary ancestry in its filename, always, never only where it collides.

✅ `term=route.guard.rung._.choice._.md` · 👎 `term=rung` · `term=guard.rung` (cut short) · `repo` at the head

boundary = SUBDOMAIN (`review.rung`), never ROLE (`driver.rung`).

test: *"$word, of WHAT?"* one word → that is `term.boundary` · only a sentence → undiscovered; record the gap in `.reason` · two answers → two senses, one cluster each.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you pave a new term | name its boundary first, then the word |
| you cannot name the boundary in one word | discovery gap, not a name gap |
| two boundaries both fit | the term does two jobs: split |
| you touch a flat term's cluster | rename on the way through, files first |
| you cite a term | cite the qualified form |
| you would rename several clusters at once | no sweep: it cannot part a citation from an instance |

a family on one ladder and verb-shape shares one boundary: settle it once. answer from the term's own `.what`, never its neighbours.

blocker: a cluster with no `term.boundary` · a flat filename on a settled term · a partial ancestry · `repo` at the head · a role as boundary · an unqualified citation · a bulk find-and-replace.
false positive: a genuinely unsettled boundary left flat with the gap recorded · an untouched extant term.
